WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.083 --> 00:00:03.294
- Devin Williams is our National League

00:00:03.294 --> 00:00:05.505
Rookie of the Year, and Harry Doyle will—
- Let's gooo!

00:00:06.047 --> 00:00:07.382
- It's an honor and a privilege to

00:00:07.382 --> 00:00:10.218
present the 2020 Jackie Robinson

00:00:10.218 --> 00:00:11.511
Rookie of the Year award in the

00:00:11.511 --> 00:00:14.723
American League to Kyle Lewis.

00:00:14.723 --> 00:00:15.974
Welcome to the club, Kyle.

00:00:16.891 --> 00:00:19.853
- Yes, sir. Thank you, guys.
Thank you, guys. Thank you, guys.

00:00:20.437 --> 00:00:21.688
You know, to have a Black hitter

00:00:21.688 --> 00:00:23.565
and a Black pitcher in the same

00:00:23.565 --> 00:00:25.859
year is something that—it’s something

00:00:25.859 --> 00:00:26.901
out of a storybook or something.

00:00:31.781 --> 00:00:34.325
- All right, gentlemen. Honored to be

00:00:34.325 --> 00:00:37.704
here with you guys, four Rookies of the Year.

00:00:37.704 --> 00:00:38.788
So, I feel like I'm left out the

00:00:38.788 --> 00:00:39.998
party, to be honest. I'm looking

00:00:40.165 --> 00:00:42.584
like, you know—I mean, Ryan, I was

00:00:42.584 --> 00:00:44.544
there with you when you won it. Doc,

00:00:44.627 --> 00:00:46.004
I was a youngster watching

00:00:46.004 --> 00:00:47.338
you, trying to be like you.

00:00:48.423 --> 00:00:49.924
You know, with the big hook and just filthy.

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:53.303
Devin, Kyle, the newest generation.

00:00:53.303 --> 00:00:56.431
The first to do it since ‘84, having two Black

00:00:56.598 --> 00:00:58.725
young men win Rookie of the Year.

00:00:58.725 --> 00:00:59.726
I mean, it's an honor.

00:01:00.226 --> 00:01:02.312
I hope you guys understand what that

00:01:02.312 --> 00:01:04.939
means to us as a community, obviously as

00:01:04.939 --> 00:01:08.026
players and role models, but, in light of

00:01:08.026 --> 00:01:09.652
everything, what that has really meant.

00:01:09.819 --> 00:01:11.446
I personally feel that winning this

00:01:11.446 --> 00:01:14.449
year had a slightly different meaning

00:01:14.449 --> 00:01:15.325
than it would

00:01:15.325 --> 00:01:16.743
have had in the past

00:01:16.743 --> 00:01:18.369
in light of everything. With that

00:01:18.369 --> 00:01:19.913
being said, it's almost like—and it’s

00:01:19.913 --> 00:01:23.666
sad that once, you know, one Rookie

00:01:23.666 --> 00:01:25.043
of the Year is announced and he's

00:01:25.543 --> 00:01:27.545
Black, he's African-American, and you

00:01:27.545 --> 00:01:28.880
know who's up in the other league,

00:01:30.173 --> 00:01:31.508
you start to think it's impossible.

00:01:31.633 --> 00:01:33.468
Like, why? Like, we've been almost

00:01:33.468 --> 00:01:36.554
trained and programmed to be, “Well,

00:01:36.554 --> 00:01:38.848
we have enough. You know, we got one.

00:01:39.099 --> 00:01:40.475
Don't ask for more than that.

00:01:41.059 --> 00:01:42.185
You got your one.

00:01:42.185 --> 00:01:43.686
So, be happy with that.”

00:01:44.771 --> 00:01:47.690
What does it mean to you to win given the

00:01:47.690 --> 00:01:50.652
environment that we're living in today?

00:01:50.652 --> 00:01:52.612
And I'll start with you, Devin.

00:01:52.612 --> 00:01:55.073
- I think for both of us from both sides of the game—

00:01:55.198 --> 00:01:56.783
you know, me on the mound and him as

00:01:56.783 --> 00:01:59.410
a position player—to be an example

00:01:59.410 --> 00:02:01.121
for these kids out here, you know,

00:02:01.121 --> 00:02:03.414
and kinda be a light and

00:02:04.082 --> 00:02:05.792
show them that this is a game

00:02:05.792 --> 00:02:07.252
that's here for them as well,

00:02:07.252 --> 00:02:09.504
I think that was a really special moment.

00:02:09.504 --> 00:02:10.380
- OK. Lew?

00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:11.714
- It means a lot for me, you

00:02:11.714 --> 00:02:13.883
know, being with my family and

00:02:13.883 --> 00:02:16.344
seeing him win. You know, as I was

00:02:16.344 --> 00:02:17.846
getting set up, you know, it

00:02:17.846 --> 00:02:19.347
brought tears to my eyes to see,

00:02:19.806 --> 00:02:21.141
especially a Black pitcher. You know,

00:02:21.141 --> 00:02:23.393
that's, you know, a rare thing, man.

00:02:23.393 --> 00:02:24.602
So, that's super special

00:02:24.602 --> 00:02:25.937
and super proud of

00:02:26.521 --> 00:02:28.231
you know, the way that we're able to

00:02:28.231 --> 00:02:31.442
represent the game. And the messages you

00:02:31.442 --> 00:02:33.695
receive after that, you know, it really

00:02:33.695 --> 00:02:35.155
means a lot. Put it into perspective,

00:02:35.697 --> 00:02:37.031
you know, even in ways I probably

00:02:37.031 --> 00:02:39.701
wasn't able to understand, you know,

00:02:39.701 --> 00:02:41.619
until afterwards. And just being able

00:02:41.619 --> 00:02:43.913
to move around in the community now, see

00:02:43.913 --> 00:02:46.249
the kids, see the reception that they

00:02:46.249 --> 00:02:48.001
have, you know, when you show up and

00:02:48.835 --> 00:02:49.919
the inspiration that they give.

00:02:49.919 --> 00:02:53.548
Yeahhh, we out here!
You know, it continues to put it

00:02:53.548 --> 00:02:54.674
into perspective every day.

00:02:54.674 --> 00:02:56.050
So, I just continue to

00:02:56.050 --> 00:02:58.178
try to carry that on.

00:02:58.178 --> 00:03:00.763
And blessed to be the next in line. Excited to

00:03:00.763 --> 00:03:02.849
see who comes next, cuz, you know, to

00:03:02.849 --> 00:03:04.475
have a Black hitter and a Black pitcher

00:03:05.059 --> 00:03:06.477
in the same year, it's just something

00:03:06.477 --> 00:03:08.897
that—it’s something out of a storybook or something.

00:03:09.564 --> 00:03:10.982
- Now, Doc, what was your experience like?

00:03:10.982 --> 00:03:12.442
I mean, you were—it hadn’t

00:03:12.442 --> 00:03:15.028
been done since the ‘84 season.

00:03:15.361 --> 00:03:16.404
Was it the same way?

00:03:16.404 --> 00:03:19.282
Like, feeling that, obviously any time

00:03:19.282 --> 00:03:20.700
we win something that, as Black

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:21.951
people—and it's done on both

00:03:21.951 --> 00:03:23.745
sides of the aisle—there's a sense of Black

00:03:23.745 --> 00:03:25.205
pride. We talk about Black pride.

00:03:25.580 --> 00:03:28.333
But it's hard to explain

00:03:28.333 --> 00:03:29.167
what it feels like.

00:03:29.167 --> 00:03:30.418
It's hard to put in words.

00:03:30.418 --> 00:03:32.295
It's something you feel, and

00:03:32.295 --> 00:03:33.671
you will never forget that feeling.

00:03:34.047 --> 00:03:36.174
So, Doc, in ‘84, when you were

00:03:36.174 --> 00:03:38.176
going through it and seeing

00:03:38.176 --> 00:03:39.594
it happen and living it,

00:03:39.886 --> 00:03:40.970
what was that experience like?

00:03:40.970 --> 00:03:43.181
And, you know, seeing it happen

00:03:43.181 --> 00:03:45.642
again so many years later, how do

00:03:45.642 --> 00:03:47.310
you feel about these two young men

00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:49.312
that went out and won the awards?

00:03:50.313 --> 00:03:52.774
- First, congratulations to both of you guys,
Devin and Kyle.

00:03:52.774 --> 00:03:54.317
I think, for me, like you said,

00:03:54.317 --> 00:03:56.361
it had been ‘84 with myself
and Alvin Davis winning it.

00:03:57.779 --> 00:03:58.780
It’s great to see, man. Cuz

00:03:58.821 --> 00:04:00.073
I'm a big fan of the game.

00:04:00.323 --> 00:04:02.325
This just puts extra, extra pride on

00:04:02.325 --> 00:04:03.910
top of it when you see two guys,

00:04:03.910 --> 00:04:05.995
especially a Black pitcher, win that award, man.

00:04:06.329 --> 00:04:07.455
And then Kyle winning it as well

00:04:07.455 --> 00:04:10.083
is just a great thing, man, to see. Proud of these guys.

00:04:10.625 --> 00:04:11.751
- Now, Big Piece.

00:04:11.751 --> 00:04:12.126
- Yes, sir.

00:04:13.503 --> 00:04:15.546
You have a history, or at least

00:04:15.546 --> 00:04:16.714
your family has a history, your

00:04:16.714 --> 00:04:18.758
parents, and, you know, maybe not a

00:04:18.758 --> 00:04:20.510
lot of people even know about this.

00:04:20.969 --> 00:04:21.928
I'm gonna leave it at that.

00:04:22.553 --> 00:04:25.348
- You know, I come from a family—

00:04:25.348 --> 00:04:26.975
my parents were both born and raised in

00:04:26.975 --> 00:04:30.144
Birmingham, Alabama. Came up in the civil

00:04:30.144 --> 00:04:32.772
rights movement. Pops was out there,

00:04:32.772 --> 00:04:35.358
you know, getting chased down by the dogs

00:04:35.358 --> 00:04:36.943
and hit with the hoses and everything.

00:04:36.943 --> 00:04:41.281
So, understanding that dynamic of,

00:04:41.281 --> 00:04:44.242
you know, where we come from as a

00:04:44.284 --> 00:04:47.537
people, that strength, that resolve,

00:04:47.537 --> 00:04:50.957
that resilience is what's helped

00:04:51.582 --> 00:04:55.670
shape me into who I am today.

00:04:56.045 --> 00:04:58.047
I try to pay it back

00:04:58.047 --> 00:04:59.716
forward as much as I can.

00:05:00.258 --> 00:05:03.011
You know, being able to talk to

00:05:03.011 --> 00:05:05.596
young players, young Black

00:05:05.596 --> 00:05:07.390
players and letting them know, because

00:05:08.016 --> 00:05:09.976
in seeing what's going on in

00:05:09.976 --> 00:05:12.395
today's society and understanding

00:05:12.687 --> 00:05:14.522
where it was, where the game was

00:05:14.522 --> 00:05:17.483
when Doc came up to where, Jimmy,

00:05:17.483 --> 00:05:18.985
where you and I came up, to where the

00:05:18.985 --> 00:05:22.280
game is with Devin and Kyle has—

00:05:22.280 --> 00:05:23.406
I mean, it's just been

00:05:23.990 --> 00:05:26.326
strides and strides and strides in

00:05:26.326 --> 00:05:27.994
the right direction currently today,

00:05:27.994 --> 00:05:30.288
because all this stuff that's going on

00:05:30.288 --> 00:05:32.790
today is stuff that's been going on.

00:05:32.790 --> 00:05:35.001
Now, the light has been shined on it

00:05:35.501 --> 00:05:37.837
even more than what it ever did because

00:05:38.129 --> 00:05:40.465
everybody's media. These cell phones tell,

00:05:40.465 --> 00:05:42.508
you know, the videos and the

00:05:42.508 --> 00:05:44.218
cell phones and all that kind of stuff.

00:05:44.635 --> 00:05:46.012
You can't hide from the video.

00:05:46.763 --> 00:05:48.306
If the video shows you doing this,

00:05:48.306 --> 00:05:49.307
they got you on video.

00:05:49.807 --> 00:05:50.308
So,

00:05:50.892 --> 00:05:54.062
I think in today's society, to your

00:05:54.062 --> 00:05:57.023
question also, Jimmy, about

00:05:57.565 --> 00:06:00.234
is the game different in baseball?

00:06:00.735 --> 00:06:03.154
I think it's different because

00:06:03.154 --> 00:06:04.739
the light has been shined on it.

00:06:05.198 --> 00:06:06.741
And you still have people today being

00:06:06.741 --> 00:06:07.909
like, “Just shut up and play.”

00:06:08.659 --> 00:06:10.828
Well, let me tell you something.

00:06:10.828 --> 00:06:13.456
I'm a man first and foremost.

00:06:13.748 --> 00:06:14.040
Yes,

00:06:14.040 --> 00:06:15.458
I put this uniform on.

00:06:15.917 --> 00:06:17.752
And yeah, this is how I pay my bills,

00:06:17.960 --> 00:06:19.754
but I'm a man first and foremost, and

00:06:19.754 --> 00:06:23.007
I damn sure wanna be treated like one.

00:06:23.966 --> 00:06:25.468
And that's what it's all about, man,

00:06:25.468 --> 00:06:27.887
is regardless of what you do—

00:06:27.887 --> 00:06:29.222
I don't care if you change oil,

00:06:29.222 --> 00:06:31.182
I don't care if you work on roofs,

00:06:31.599 --> 00:06:33.559
man. You are a man, and you are a woman,

00:06:33.976 --> 00:06:35.686
and you should be treated as a human

00:06:35.686 --> 00:06:37.814
being with all kind of respects,

00:06:38.106 --> 00:06:40.108
regardless of what that paycheck looks like.

00:06:40.108 --> 00:06:43.653
- Devin and Kyle,
how did what happened this summer,

00:06:43.653 --> 00:06:44.695
Black Lives Matter,

00:06:45.238 --> 00:06:47.698
how did that connect everyone?

00:06:48.241 --> 00:06:49.492
Obviously we know about the Players

00:06:49.492 --> 00:06:51.411
Alliance, how that all came about.

00:06:51.411 --> 00:06:53.121
Now, we're all in on a group, whether

00:06:53.121 --> 00:06:54.914
we’re out of the game, coming to

00:06:54.914 --> 00:06:57.166
the end or just starting. But how did

00:06:57.166 --> 00:07:00.169
you guys as Black players find

00:07:00.169 --> 00:07:02.588
one another, connect, communicate

00:07:03.131 --> 00:07:05.675
and say, you know, “What can we do?”

00:07:06.008 --> 00:07:06.259
- Yeah.

00:07:06.259 --> 00:07:09.262
I think it just, it brought the Black

00:07:09.262 --> 00:07:11.722
community and baseball closer together.

00:07:11.722 --> 00:07:12.265
You know what I mean?

00:07:12.265 --> 00:07:13.850
Like, everyone was just kind

00:07:13.850 --> 00:07:14.809
of there for each other.

00:07:14.809 --> 00:07:16.811
You know, if you need something,

00:07:16.811 --> 00:07:17.854
you can always reach out.

00:07:17.854 --> 00:07:19.522
Like, I mean, I can't even tell you how

00:07:19.522 --> 00:07:21.274
many texts I got like that from older

00:07:21.274 --> 00:07:23.568
players, like, in the game or retired

00:07:23.568 --> 00:07:25.653
guys. Like, and I mean, it was just

00:07:25.653 --> 00:07:27.196
a good support system for me to have,

00:07:27.196 --> 00:07:28.948
especially being a rookie, you know?

00:07:29.407 --> 00:07:31.701
Being the only Black guy in my

00:07:31.701 --> 00:07:34.328
clubhouse since LoCain, he opted out.

00:07:34.328 --> 00:07:35.830
So, I was the only Black guy.

00:07:36.289 --> 00:07:38.249
It was good to have people like CC

00:07:38.249 --> 00:07:39.917
and some others, like, reach out to

00:07:39.917 --> 00:07:42.211
me, you know, just supporting.

00:07:42.211 --> 00:07:44.338
- You know, I give a lot of credit to, you know, the

00:07:44.338 --> 00:07:46.549
club, you know, for doing what they

00:07:46.549 --> 00:07:48.551
could as well, far as making efforts.

00:07:48.551 --> 00:07:51.888
And me and Devin met on a Zoom

00:07:51.888 --> 00:07:53.848
call, you know, speaking about being

00:07:53.848 --> 00:07:55.600
Black in baseball, you know,

00:07:55.600 --> 00:07:56.893
different experiences that we had

00:07:56.893 --> 00:07:59.437
had, you know, growing up and then speaking

00:07:59.437 --> 00:08:01.063
about it and just trying to connect,

00:08:01.564 --> 00:08:02.773
you know, the African Americans in the

00:08:02.773 --> 00:08:04.442
game the best way we knew

00:08:04.442 --> 00:08:06.569
how. So, I'm appreciative of them

00:08:06.569 --> 00:08:07.904
for setting that up as well.

00:08:08.362 --> 00:08:09.238
You know, there's a lot of different

00:08:09.238 --> 00:08:12.325
avenues that, you know, started to show

00:08:12.325 --> 00:08:14.368
themselves, whether it be group chats,

00:08:14.368 --> 00:08:16.078
whether it be meetings in the hotel.

00:08:16.370 --> 00:08:18.164
And, you know, I think guys really started to

00:08:18.623 --> 00:08:20.458
understand the magnitude and put that

00:08:20.458 --> 00:08:23.461
sense of urgency on us sticking together and

00:08:23.461 --> 00:08:26.005
having each other's back, you know? And it

00:08:26.005 --> 00:08:27.465
really started to show itself throughout

00:08:27.465 --> 00:08:29.258
the year in a lot of different ways.

00:08:29.634 --> 00:08:31.010
- And, Doc, I wanna go back to you.

00:08:31.010 --> 00:08:32.887
Because, you know, you're the generation

00:08:32.887 --> 00:08:36.182
before me, before Ryan, before all of us.

00:08:36.182 --> 00:08:38.893
The activism that is allowed

00:08:38.893 --> 00:08:40.686
today versus back in the ‘80s,

00:08:41.187 --> 00:08:43.397
what is the difference that you see,

00:08:43.481 --> 00:08:45.608
that you lived through then and that,

00:08:45.650 --> 00:08:46.943
although you're not in uniform, you live

00:08:46.943 --> 00:08:48.486
through because you're a Black man today?

00:08:49.111 --> 00:08:51.197
- If we’re talking ‘86, the guys on

00:08:51.197 --> 00:08:52.657
our team was pretty close, but at that

00:08:52.657 --> 00:08:54.200
time, it's just a different generation.

00:08:54.200 --> 00:08:56.077
We had some situations where it

00:08:56.077 --> 00:08:57.370
kind of got heated, you know, with

00:08:57.370 --> 00:08:58.496
racial comments being made on the

00:08:58.496 --> 00:09:00.039
bus from alcohol or what have you.

00:09:00.623 --> 00:09:01.624
And they’d say they apologized,

00:09:01.624 --> 00:09:04.627
but it's totally different now.
I can see the culture is now.

00:09:04.627 --> 00:09:06.379
It is different, and

00:09:06.379 --> 00:09:07.380
we did have more Blacks,

00:09:07.380 --> 00:09:09.382
I think, in my generation. I

00:09:09.382 --> 00:09:11.509
came up in ‘84 with teams and

00:09:11.509 --> 00:09:12.843
different teams. The way it is now,

00:09:12.843 --> 00:09:14.303
I think now it's almost

00:09:14.303 --> 00:09:16.138
like, one to a team, I think I heard.

00:09:16.305 --> 00:09:17.974
Totally different, but probably

00:09:17.974 --> 00:09:19.934
more difficult, I would think, now than what

00:09:19.934 --> 00:09:21.269
we had, because we did have at least

00:09:21.269 --> 00:09:22.853
three or four that we could hang out

00:09:22.853 --> 00:09:25.773
with at times on the road or

00:09:25.773 --> 00:09:26.941
just have those conversations.

00:09:26.941 --> 00:09:27.942
You know, just talking, you know,

00:09:27.942 --> 00:09:29.110
nothing serious or

00:09:29.110 --> 00:09:31.821
anything like that, but sometimes we

00:09:31.821 --> 00:09:33.698
might just want to talk about certain things.

00:09:34.156 --> 00:09:35.241
We had four or five guys

00:09:35.241 --> 00:09:37.159
you could do that with, where now, you

00:09:37.159 --> 00:09:38.411
know, these guys, they probably know

00:09:38.411 --> 00:09:39.579
better than I do, but it don’t seem

00:09:39.579 --> 00:09:40.663
like there’s that many on the teams.

00:09:40.955 --> 00:09:44.917
Some teams don't even have any. You know, they 
have the Latin ballplayers, but not the Black ballplayers.

00:09:45.459 --> 00:09:49.880
- I wanna go here next with our, baseball's

00:09:49.880 --> 00:09:52.258
version of Colin Kaepernick,

00:09:52.675 --> 00:09:54.135
and that’s Bruce Maxwell,

00:09:54.135 --> 00:09:58.264
who, much like you, Devin, was solo.

00:09:58.264 --> 00:10:00.558
I mean, one brother on a team.

00:10:01.726 --> 00:10:03.477
A clubhouse full of people that

00:10:03.477 --> 00:10:05.730
don't really understand the impact

00:10:05.730 --> 00:10:06.897
of what's happening

00:10:06.897 --> 00:10:07.982
in today to Black men to

00:10:07.982 --> 00:10:09.692
every time we leave the house,

00:10:09.692 --> 00:10:11.152
we're looking over our shoulder.

00:10:11.152 --> 00:10:12.153
We're in the big leagues.

00:10:12.153 --> 00:10:14.280
We make money. We see

00:10:14.280 --> 00:10:16.324
lights, we still get nervous.

00:10:16.782 --> 00:10:18.743
It doesn't matter how much money we have.

00:10:19.285 --> 00:10:19.994
It doesn't matter how

00:10:19.994 --> 00:10:21.203
much we've accomplished.

00:10:21.454 --> 00:10:24.290
When we take off and leave our houses,

00:10:24.707 --> 00:10:26.792
we know all they see is the skin color.

00:10:27.209 --> 00:10:29.003
I might be the nicest person in the world.

00:10:29.003 --> 00:10:29.879
I might be the meanest

00:10:29.879 --> 00:10:30.588
person in the world.

00:10:30.588 --> 00:10:31.213
It doesn't matter.

00:10:31.213 --> 00:10:32.340
That skin color means

00:10:32.340 --> 00:10:33.758
something to everyone else.

00:10:34.175 --> 00:10:35.635
So, then you have Bruce Maxwell, who

00:10:35.635 --> 00:10:37.720
decides, “I'm gonna take a knee

00:10:37.720 --> 00:10:39.180
during the national anthem” and

00:10:39.180 --> 00:10:40.389
stand up for what he believes in.

00:10:41.098 --> 00:10:41.641
And,

00:10:42.308 --> 00:10:43.851
to be honest, no one

00:10:43.851 --> 00:10:46.520
had his back. As rookies,

00:10:46.520 --> 00:10:48.022
I don't expect you guys to come out and

00:10:48.022 --> 00:10:49.857
to say anything. That's—

00:10:50.441 --> 00:10:51.609
it's kinda hard to say that's not

00:10:51.609 --> 00:10:55.946
your place, but it's kind of not your

00:10:55.946 --> 00:10:57.657
place to be on the forefront of that.

00:10:57.657 --> 00:10:59.450
But he had to do something that

00:10:59.450 --> 00:11:02.203
he believed in. A rookie, you know,

00:11:02.370 --> 00:11:05.748
taking the knee, close to San Francisco,

00:11:05.748 --> 00:11:07.625
to Oakland, Bay Area, where it

00:11:07.625 --> 00:11:08.751
happened with Colin Kaepernick.

00:11:09.043 --> 00:11:11.545
How do you feel about the way

00:11:11.545 --> 00:11:13.631
he was sacrificed and having an

00:11:13.631 --> 00:11:14.674
opportunity to come back to the

00:11:14.674 --> 00:11:16.384
A's and saying, “I don't want it”?

00:11:16.967 --> 00:11:18.761
- I just think, you know, at that

00:11:18.761 --> 00:11:20.763
time, guys were scared.

00:11:20.763 --> 00:11:21.514
You know what I mean?

00:11:21.514 --> 00:11:22.556
Scared to support that

00:11:23.099 --> 00:11:24.141
because of the backlash.

00:11:24.141 --> 00:11:25.434
I mean, you saw what happened with him and

00:11:25.434 --> 00:11:27.228
you saw what happened with Kaepernick. But

00:11:27.895 --> 00:11:29.063
we don't really hear about him that

00:11:29.063 --> 00:11:30.856
much because he's not, he's not the

00:11:30.856 --> 00:11:33.067
name that Kaepernick is. Like, he kind

00:11:33.067 --> 00:11:35.111
of just got pushed to the side and

00:11:35.111 --> 00:11:37.780
forgotten about. And, you know,

00:11:37.780 --> 00:11:41.283
that's really sad to see, but I also

00:11:41.283 --> 00:11:43.160
really respect the fact that he

00:11:43.160 --> 00:11:45.079
declined the offer to come back.

00:11:45.079 --> 00:11:47.206
- I think it did set an example for,

00:11:47.873 --> 00:11:49.709
you know, this year being able to,

00:11:50.835 --> 00:11:53.212
you know, understand the magnitude of

00:11:53.212 --> 00:11:55.548
a decision like that and being able

00:11:55.548 --> 00:11:57.675
to connect with other players, you know,

00:11:57.675 --> 00:11:59.093
to prepare, to try to

00:11:59.093 --> 00:12:00.553
make a unified stance.

00:12:01.011 --> 00:12:02.138
You know, I think it definitely

00:12:02.138 --> 00:12:03.472
helped pave the way for that.

00:12:03.472 --> 00:12:05.182
And, so, you know, that's something that's

00:12:05.182 --> 00:12:07.393
always valuable. You know, moving forward

00:12:07.393 --> 00:12:10.104
is us understanding that, you know, when

00:12:10.104 --> 00:12:12.606
one of us wants to try to make a stance,

00:12:13.149 --> 00:12:14.567
you know, let's come together and let's

00:12:14.567 --> 00:12:16.569
try to do it the right way, in a way that,

00:12:17.111 --> 00:12:18.821
you know, is gonna be heard with unity

00:12:18.821 --> 00:12:21.407
so that one player can't be, you

00:12:21.407 --> 00:12:24.285
know, painted as an outlier or,

00:12:24.285 --> 00:12:25.703
you know, pushed to the side.

00:12:25.703 --> 00:12:27.246
- You know, for us, it started with

00:12:27.246 --> 00:12:28.748
Jackie Robinson having to go

00:12:28.748 --> 00:12:31.417
through and take all the abuse and

00:12:32.001 --> 00:12:34.545
have the courage to not fight back.

00:12:35.713 --> 00:12:37.131
Although he fought back in different ways,

00:12:37.131 --> 00:12:39.049
but not to physically go on a field and

00:12:39.467 --> 00:12:40.551
knock somebody’s teeth out,

00:12:40.551 --> 00:12:42.136
cuz you know he wanted to do that.

00:12:42.136 --> 00:12:43.304
Jackie wasn't a soft dude.

00:12:43.304 --> 00:12:44.221
Let's just make that

00:12:44.221 --> 00:12:47.558
clear. But going with the history

00:12:47.558 --> 00:12:48.976
of baseball from that time

00:12:48.976 --> 00:12:51.437
to Blacks just having an explosion in baseball

00:12:51.437 --> 00:12:53.647
to dwindling down to now you see teams

00:12:53.647 --> 00:12:56.776
with not even one Black player on it.

00:12:57.485 --> 00:12:58.652
I've asked myself, you know,

00:12:58.652 --> 00:12:59.987
would I have taken a knee

00:12:59.987 --> 00:13:01.322
had I been a current player?

00:13:02.156 --> 00:13:03.741
And honestly, I don't know.

00:13:04.158 --> 00:13:06.285
But the reality of it is, we all

00:13:06.285 --> 00:13:07.244
have to answer that question.

00:13:07.244 --> 00:13:09.038
“If I do this, what will

00:13:09.038 --> 00:13:10.039
be the consequence?”

00:13:10.289 --> 00:13:12.249
So, to be out there without that

00:13:12.249 --> 00:13:14.752
support, hoping that just, you know,

00:13:14.752 --> 00:13:16.170
because you're standing up for a cause,

00:13:16.170 --> 00:13:17.379
that everyone is gonna have your

00:13:17.379 --> 00:13:19.882
back, that “I'm gonna to do this for us.”

00:13:20.174 --> 00:13:21.967
And then to find out on

00:13:21.967 --> 00:13:23.302
the other side, everybody

00:13:24.178 --> 00:13:26.055
Is looking out for themselves.

00:13:26.597 --> 00:13:27.765
Even if that's not true,

00:13:27.765 --> 00:13:28.808
that's what it feels like.

00:13:28.808 --> 00:13:30.476
You're out there on your own.

00:13:30.768 --> 00:13:32.520
Had that not happened to Kaepernick

00:13:32.520 --> 00:13:33.896
and then Bruce and then everything

00:13:33.896 --> 00:13:35.898
that happened, would baseball’s activism—

00:13:35.898 --> 00:13:39.109
activism in baseball even existed 
like it is today?

00:13:39.485 --> 00:13:40.486
- No self-pity with me, but

00:13:40.486 --> 00:13:42.905
the thing that happened with me and
Sheff in Tampa, when

00:13:42.905 --> 00:13:44.198
that stuff took place, and the thing that

00:13:44.198 --> 00:13:46.200
hurt me the most, it was not that

00:13:46.200 --> 00:13:48.494
this incident that happened.

00:13:48.494 --> 00:13:52.998
But everybody was saying that, “Oh, you
must have done something wrong.” Cuz we're

00:13:52.998 --> 00:13:54.792
Black, we must've done something wrong.

00:13:54.792 --> 00:13:56.502
That part bothered me. And a lot of this stuff

00:13:56.502 --> 00:13:57.795
took place, happening just to

00:13:57.795 --> 00:14:00.506
bring attention to what’s going on.

00:14:00.923 --> 00:14:02.383
And unfortunately, we had a couple

00:14:02.383 --> 00:14:03.300
of brothers lose their lives.

00:14:03.926 --> 00:14:05.719
You know, with Floyd and

00:14:05.719 --> 00:14:07.805
others, to get the attention. And

00:14:07.805 --> 00:14:10.516
then with the protesting, the

00:14:10.516 --> 00:14:12.393
marches, and having the white people march

00:14:12.393 --> 00:14:14.562
also, bringing attention to

00:14:14.562 --> 00:14:15.312
what's going on.

00:14:15.312 --> 00:14:17.731
I’d say now, just doing what you’re comfortable

00:14:17.731 --> 00:14:19.149
with doing. I don't think we

00:14:19.149 --> 00:14:22.987
have to do anything—I’m just speaking
really for myself. You gotta do something—

00:14:22.987 --> 00:14:25.239
before y’all do it because you think you gotta do it,

00:14:25.239 --> 00:14:26.949
do what you feel comfortable with
in your own skin and

00:14:26.949 --> 00:14:28.325
understand that before you do something,

00:14:29.243 --> 00:14:30.286
“Is this something I really want to

00:14:30.286 --> 00:14:33.289
do?” and consequences that come behind that,

00:14:33.289 --> 00:14:34.456
whether it's good consequences or

00:14:34.456 --> 00:14:36.542
bad consequences and understand that.
And asking yourself,

00:14:36.542 --> 00:14:38.377
“Am I gonna be OK

00:14:38.377 --> 00:14:39.670
to live with this? Is my family gonna
be OK to live with this?”

00:14:39.670 --> 00:14:41.839
Then you take the stance.

00:14:42.256 --> 00:14:42.882
But you still want you to

00:14:42.882 --> 00:14:43.757
feel what’s right in your heart.

00:14:44.258 --> 00:14:44.884
Carry on the message.

